  THE TWO ROWS AT BM=64 BN=64 G=16 ARE NOT ONE CELL and the table does not
  pretend they are: bn64-g16-m64 ran at num_stages=4 in the bn_decomposition arm and
  s3w8g16-m64 at num_stages=3 in the occupancy arm, which is why their slopes are
  0.57804 and 0.59157 ms per tile. Each contrast is read WITHIN one arm, so the
  pipeline depth is fixed inside A and inside B and the two ratios are not compared with each other.

  WHAT NEITHER CONTRAST CAN DO. A DRAM counter sees the weight re-read and the
  activation re-read in one number, so it cannot split dR/dn into alpha_b and
  alpha_a. It settles whether the term is traffic; it does not name the operand.

THE COMMAND, on a box where --probe says OPEN.

  # 1. the timed ladder, unprofiled, so time and traffic come from the same
  #    cell. It KEEPS the software L2 flush, because this one is the timed
  #    apparatus and the flush is part of it; only the profiled runs in step 2
  #    turn it off, where ncu's --cache-control sets the cache state instead
  #    and the flush would otherwise put its own 240 MB into the counter.
  #    Only the primary cell needs this: the contrast cells' timed ladders are
  #    already in results/published/2026-09-10-nvidia_h200-gaps-session and are
  #    what the predictions above were fitted from.
  python scripts/block_m_crossing_sweep.py --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 \
      --tiles 64 --group-m 16 --block-n 64 \
      --num-stages 4 \
      --r-max 512 --row-step 64 --step-probes 0

  # 2. the counter itself. --run drives ncu, counts the calls out of the
  #    profile's own launch list and writes the schema below. It is a MODE
  #    and not a shell loop because of that count: the instrument
  #    (moe/bench/timing.time_kernel) warms up for a DURATION, --warmup in
  #    MILLISECONDS, and sizes its own iteration count from that load, so the
  #    profiled launch count is warmup + iters x trials fused_experts calls of
  #    several kernels each and every byte field must be divided by it.
  #    Dividing by an assumed 1 still fits an affine line and still passes the
  #    residual and monotonicity gates, so nothing downstream would catch it.
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --run --card nvidia_h200 \
      --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 --block-m 64 --block-n 32 \
      --group-m 16 --num-stages 4 --tiles 1,2,3,4,6,8 \
      --cache-control all --out counters-bn32-g16-m64-ccall.json   # BN-low
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --run --card nvidia_h200 \
      --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 --block-m 64 --block-n 64 \
      --group-m 16 --num-stages 4 --tiles 1,2,3,4,6,8 \
      --cache-control all --out counters-bn64-g16-m64-ccall.json   # BN-hinge, the plan's primary cell
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --run --card nvidia_h200 \
      --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 --block-m 64 --block-n 128 \
      --group-m 16 --num-stages 4 --tiles 1,2,3,4,6,8 \
      --cache-control all --out counters-bn128-g16-m64-ccall.json   # BN-high
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --run --card nvidia_h200 \
      --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 --block-m 64 --block-n 64 \
      --group-m 1 --num-stages 3 --tiles 1,2,3,4,6,8 \
      --cache-control all --out counters-s3w8g1-m64-ccall.json   # schedule: no swizzle
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --run --card nvidia_h200 \
      --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 --block-m 64 --block-n 64 \
      --group-m 16 --num-stages 3 --tiles 1,2,3,4,6,8 \
      --cache-control all --out counters-s3w8g16-m64-ccall.json   # schedule: the shipped swizzle

  # 3. score ONE cell. --run scores its own file as it writes it; --analyse
  #    rescores one anybody wrote, and refuses a payload with a key missing
  #    rather than defaulting it to a zero that was never measured.
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --analyse counters-<cell>.json

  # 4. score the CONTRAST, which is the reading this plan exists to take and
  #    which no single payload contains: the discriminator is a RATIO of
  #    dR/dn across two cells and step 3 scores one cell at a time. Pairs are
  #    matched inside one cache mode, the measured ratio is compared with the
  #    registered rivals above, and a pair nobody ran is not scored.
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --contrast counters-bn32-g16-m64-ccall.json counters-bn64-g16-m64-ccall.json counters-bn128-g16-m64-ccall.json counters-s3w8g1-m64-ccall.json counters-s3w8g16-m64-ccall.json

CACHE CONTROL, and why it is a swept parameter rather than a default.
  ncu's default is Flush All: all GPU caches are flushed before each replay
  iteration. That is a DIFFERENT cache state from the timed sweep, which never
  flushes. The LEVEL it can move is bounded by L2, and L2 is 40-60 MB against a
  2.82 GB weight read -- at most 2.1% of R(1). The SLOPE,
  which is the claim, differences that term away entirely. Run both modes
  anyway: two agreeing numbers close the question, and two disagreeing ones
  are themselves the result.

COST, of the plan as extended.
  5 cells x 6 tile counts x 2 cache modes = 60 profiled invocations
  of at least 11 fused_experts calls each, about 3300 profiled kernel launches.
  The largest cell is n=8 (512 rows per expert), which the timed
  sweep measures in single-digit milliseconds; ncu replay and its save/restore
  of the 2.8 GB weight buffers dominate, at seconds per launch.
  At 5 minutes per profiled invocation that is 5.0 GPU-hours for the whole
  extended plan, against 1.0 for the single cell the plan used to hold, and
  about half again in pod time. Not the fifteen minutes the one-launch recipe
  used to promise. At H200 spot rates that is tens of dollars. The cost of
  this experiment has never been the money.
  DROP TO 36 INVOCATIONS (3.0 GPU-hours) by running contrast A alone: the
  three BLOCK_N cells settle section 2. Contrast B is the schedule question and
  is worth its own two cells only once A has an answer.

WHICH KERNELS TO KEEP, and why the profile is NOT name-filtered.
  All of W moves inside the two `fused_moe_kernel` launches; the auxiliary
  launches (align, reduction) move activation-sized traffic only. Filtering to
  the GEMM would still be defensible -- but then the measured level would no
  longer be comparable to the byte model, which charges the whole layer. So
  profile everything, record bytes PER KERNEL NAME, and let the analysis
  report both totals. A filter applied at capture cannot be undone afterwards.

SCHEMA the run must write, so --analyse can score it:
{
  "device": "nvidia_h200",       # the card slug, matching the calibration file
  "model": "mixtral-8x7b", "dtype": "bf16",
  "group_m": 16, "block_n": 64, "block_k": 64,
  "num_warps": 8, "num_stages": 4, "block_m": 64,
  "cache_control": "all",           # ncu --cache-control; "all" or "none"
  "ridge": 151.43,                # THIS card's own calibration, never 152.8
  "anchors":  {"published": 0.6595, "t1": 0.6202, "n3": 0.6583},
  "bracket":  [0.6202, 0.9753],
  "rows": [ {"n": 1, "calls": 11, "calls_floor": 10, "launches": 55,
             "dram_bytes_read": 2.84e9,
             "dram_bytes_write": 1.1e8, "l2_read_hit_pct": 4.2,
             "gpu_time_ns": 1.95e6,
             "by_kernel": {"fused_moe_kernel": 2.81e9, "moe_sum": 3.0e7} }, ... ]
}

  Every key is required. `--analyse` raises on a missing one rather than
  defaulting it: a zero that was never measured is the failure mode this whole
  file exists to avoid, and `--run` refuses a metric ncu did not return rather
  than writing a zero into the row. `calls` is the number of fused_experts
  calls the instrument made (warmup + iters x trials), COUNTED from the
  profile's own launch list as the launches of `moe_align_block_size`, which vLLM runs
  exactly once per call; `calls_floor` is that cell's own iters x trials, read
  back from the row the sweep wrote, and `calls` must EXCEED it because warmup
  adds calls on top. `launches` is every profiled kernel launch. The byte
  fields are PER CALL, the sums over all launches divided by `calls`, never by
  an assumed one.

  `block_k` and `num_warps` are the sweep's own FIXED values, imported rather
  than typed: this block said num_stages 3 while `block_m_crossing_sweep.FIXED`
  said 4 and the recipe passed no --num-stages, so the schema described a cell
  the recipe would not have run.

